---
/**
 * About.
 *
 * Deliberately about principles rather than biography — the founder's own story
 * is the one thing this page cannot write for him. The TODO block below is his
 * to fill; everything else here is true as written.
 */
import Public from '../layouts/Public.astro'
import { coverage } from '../lib/site.ts'

const card = 'rounded-xl border border-ink-100 bg-white p-5'

const principles = [
  {
    title: 'We show you the rule',
    body: 'Every deadline names the rule it comes from. If we say your MCS-150 is due in October, we show you the rule is 49 CFR 390.19T, so you can check it. You should not have to trust a compliance product that you cannot check.',
  },
  {
    title: 'We tell you when we do not know',
    body: 'If we do not have the date on a driver medical card, we show it as unknown and treat it as urgent, the same as overdue. Your risk is the same either way. We will not show you a date we guessed.',
  },
  {
    title: 'We do not use numbers we cannot check',
    body: 'Companies in this industry repeat fines that are not in the rules. The most common one is ten thousand dollars a day for not having a testing program. That number is not in the penalty schedule. The real numbers are high enough.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Your records are yours',
    body: 'We do not sell your data. We do not share it with brokers or insurance companies, unless you send them a proof link yourself. If you leave, you take everything with you.',
  },
]
---

<Public
  title="About"
  description="Why FleetView Compliance exists and how it is built."
>
  <section class="border-b border-ink-100 bg-ink-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6 sm:py-20">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">
        Why we built FleetView.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-500">
        A carrier with 12 trucks has to follow the same rules as a carrier with 500 trucks, but
        with no staff to do it. Software built for the big fleets costs more than a truck payment
        and needs a full-time person to run it. So most small fleets use a spreadsheet and a wall
        calendar. That works until the day it does not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How we build it</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      {
        principles.map((p) => (
          <div class={card}>
            <p class="font-medium">{p.title}</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-500">{p.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Federal everywhere, California first</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-500">
      Most of what a carrier owes is federal, and federal rules are the same in every state.{' '}
      <strong class="tabular-nums">{coverage.federal}</strong> of the{' '}
      <strong class="tabular-nums">{coverage.total}</strong> rules on our list are the same
      wherever your trucks are based. Every carrier gets all of them.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-500">
      Most national software stops there. But a California carrier also has to meet CARB Clean
      Truck Check, a CHP terminal inspection program that moved to a 90-day cycle in January
      2026, a Motor Carrier Permit on its own schedule, and CVRA weight decals. These are the
      ones that put a hold on your registration, and they are the ones most software leaves out.
      So California is the state we built first. Those are the other{' '}
      <strong class="tabular-nums">{coverage.california}</strong> rules.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-500">
      California is also the only state we have done so far. We say that on the home page, not in
      small print at the bottom. We would rather do one state well than fifty badly, and we will
      not list a state we have not done. The next states will be quicker because of a choice we
      made on day one: for us a rule is <em>data</em>, not code. A rule is the rule number, the
      schedule, what proves you did it, and what happens if you miss it. Adding California meant
      adding rules to a list. The next state has to work the same way.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-brand-500/30 bg-brand-500/[0.04] p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Talk to a person</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-ink-500">
        We are small. When you write to us, you reach someone who built this.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/check"
        class="mt-5 inline-block rounded-xl bg-ink-900 px-7 py-3.5 font-semibold text-white transition hover:bg-ink-700"
      >
        Start with your DOT number →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Public>
